> I like this idea. Weaviate could rewrite the query under the hood to replace a where filter with a partition value, etc. A possible downside would be that this could complicate other APIs. Right now they're quite simple, with just (using HTTP query vars as an example) ?partion=foo. If the portion becomes a key-value pair we might need something like ?partition=my_particionkey=foo.

We could set the property name in the Class schema via an option:

```
{
  /* ... other fields ... */
  "partitioning": {
    "enabled": true,
    "property": "tenant_id"
  }
}
```

So in your second example it would still be just `partition=foo`. I think this feature though could be added afterwards as is more a UX thing.

> I can see usecases where users have access to multiple tenants, for example their personal-documents tenant and one or more team-document tenants that should be searched at the same time.

One of the design principles above was that this should never be possible, i.e. strict isolation between tenants. I'm curious how users would model that. Maybe @participant3 can shed some light as well. I could imagine that a common approach would be to have the "organization" (or "team", "group", etc) be the tenant and then have individual user permissions regulated through specific fields and filters.

 @participant1 might also have some insights into how other multi-tenancy cases treat this. I do remember that the user/group topic came up in a customer conversation at some point.

The Proposal API's Schema section has been updated. The previously named `partitioning` field has been renamed `multiTenancyConfig`, and `partitionKey` to `tenantKey`, to make it more clear to the end user what these fields are for.

Additionally, we introduce the limitation of `tenantKey` property type to be either `text` or `uuid`.

---

```js
{
  /* ... other fields ... */
  "multiTenancyConfig": {
    "tenantKey": "name of the class property to be used as tenant key",
    "enabled": true|false
  },
  "shardingConfig": null,
}

```

In the initial implementation, the `tenantKey` will be restricted to properties of type `text` and `uuid`. This can be expanded to include other property types in future iterations.

---
